Part 1: Overview
Welcome back to Hudson Mobility Analytics. The dataset you built in Part 1 has landed, and the operations team's question is now on your desk: can we predict how many rides will happen on a given day, using nothing but the calendar and the weather forecast?
In this Tlab you will carry your CSV through the full analysis workflow you've practiced throughout each phase: explore it, find and fix its quality problems, engineer the features a linear model needs, then build, evaluate, and interpret a Linear Regression model of daily ridership.
A warning from your manager: this dataset looks clean. It is not. Real-world data rarely announces its problems. Part of your grade rests on whether your EDA finds them before your model does.

Part 2: Environment Setup
Open your project repository in GitHub Codespaces as usual.
Your raw data is the file you committed in Part 1: data/citibike_weather_daily.csv. If your Part 1 export had problems, fix it first — everything here depends on it.
You created three empty notebooks in your code/ folder back in Part 1. Now you fill them, in this order: eda.ipynb, preprocessing.ipynb, model.ipynb. Each must run top to bottom without errors.
preprocessing.ipynb must end by saving your cleaned dataset to data/citibike_weather_daily_clean.csv. model.ipynb loads the clean file and never touches the raw one.

