Part 4: Business Scenario & Rules of the Road
The Scenario: “Here's the ask,” your manager says. “Ops wants a number every morning: how many rides should we expect today? They'll have the calendar and the weather forecast — nothing else. Build me a model that turns those into a prediction, and more importantly, be ready to tell me what it gets wrong and why.”
Your prediction target is num_rides. Two rules are graded, so read them now:
Rule 1 — Features must be knowable in advance. Every feature must be something ops could know from the calendar and a weather forecast before the day starts. That rules out anything derived from that day's rides (including avg_duration_min) and anything from previous days' ride counts. No “yesterday's rides” features.
Rule 2 — Don't stack the three temperature columns. temp_f, max_temp_f, and min_temp_f move together almost perfectly. Do not put all three in your model as if they were independent. Choose one (or derive one feature from them) and explain your choice in a markdown cell. If you're curious why, try all three and watch what happens to the coefficients — then write about it.
